# Example Caddy config for the reverse proxy in front of nice4iot.
#
# The compose files here do NOT ship a proxy: nice4iot joins the external
# `proxy` network and only `expose`s 8080 to it, so this belongs in whatever
# runs your proxy. Mount it there as /etc/caddy/Caddyfile and put that
# container on the same `proxy` network, so `nice4iot:8080` resolves.
#
# Two listeners, for two very different clients:
#   - the HTTPS site  — humans (admin UI) and devices (REST API), everything
#   - plain HTTP :8081 — e-paper displays, image.png only, LAN only
# See "Serving display images over plain HTTP" in deploy/README.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTTPS — the normal, complete entry point. Caddy provisions the certificate.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
iot.example.com {
	reverse_proxy nice4iot:8080
}

# To serve under a sub-path instead (nice4iot then needs `--root-path /iot`,
# see deploy/README.md), replace the block above with:
#
#iot.example.com {
#	redir /iot /iot/          # so relative asset URLs resolve
#	handle_path /iot/* {
#		reverse_proxy nice4iot:8080
#	}
#}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plain HTTP for e-paper displays — images only, LAN only.
#
# E-paper firmware usually has no certificate store worth the name, so a
# deployment that is HTTPS-only can be unreachable for the very devices the
# images exist for. This second listener serves nothing but the rendered PNG,
# restricted to the display network. Both conditions are ANDed; everything
# else on this port — the admin UI, the rest of the API, requests from off the
# LAN — gets a flat 404.
#
# Adapt 192.168.2.0/24 to the network your displays sit on. The path pattern
# matches the epaper extension's route
#   /api/ext/epaper/<project>/screens/<screen>/image.png
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
http://:8081 {
	@image {
		remote_ip 192.168.2.0/24
		path_regexp ^/api/ext/epaper/[^/]+/screens/[^/]+/image\.png$
	}
	handle @image {
		reverse_proxy nice4iot:8080
	}
	respond 404
}

# This ADDS a way to reach the images, it does not move them: image.png stays
# available over HTTPS on the site above, subject to whatever protects it
# there. That is intended — the screen editor loads its preview from its own
# origin with a relative URL, so excluding the image path from the HTTPS site
# to "have only one way in" breaks the preview.
#
# What it does and does not buy you:
#   - Not confidentiality. The images travel unencrypted and unauthenticated;
#     anyone on that LAN can fetch any screen of any project. Fine for a
#     weather panel, a deliberate decision for a room calendar whose rendered
#     image shows meeting subjects.
#   - remote_ip is not authentication. It matches the directly connecting
#     peer, which anything on the same LAN can hold. It keeps the plain port
#     off the internet; it does not keep a compromised device off it.
#   - The editor views come along: ?raw=true and ?boxes=true live on the same
#     path. Add `not query raw=*` and `not query boxes=*` to @image if that
#     bothers you.
#   - Device bearer tokens are unaffected — this route needs none, and adding
#     it does not weaken the token auth on the rest of /api.
